Hello again,
=20
Further to my original question, I received the following response from =
Steve Parker (UK)
=20
***********
I have used a DECserver 90M where you can set-up a port to act as a =
telnet port.
=20
For example,  Port 1 can be stup as telnet port 2001, Port 2 as telnet =
port 2002 etc.
=20
The commands are:
=20
change port 1 access remote
change port 1 default protocol telnet
change port 1 session limit 1
change tel list 2001 port 1
change tel list 2001 connections enabled
=20
=20
You can then use a terminal or terminal emulator to 'telnet' to that =
port rather than the default telnet port 23.
=20
e.g if the ip address of the terminal server was 192.12.12.12 and one of =
you routers was connected to port 1, you could telnet to IP address =
192.12.12.12 port 2001 and gain access to the device connected to the =
port.
=20
i.e. telnet 192.12.12.12 2001
=20
Regards
=20
Steve Parker
********************
It worked on my DECserver 900TM as well. My 4-wire cable connecting the =
DECserver and the router was alright. No flow control is necessary.=20
=20
Thank you, Steve and Bye for now,
=20
Hung

